# Project: JUnit 5 Java Test Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use JUnit 5 (JUnit Jupiter) as the primary test runner for Java projects
- Manage dependencies with Maven or Gradle; prefer JUnit BOM for version alignment
- Organize tests under src/test/java mirroring the main source package structure
- Separate unit, integration, and system tests into distinct source sets or packages

## Coding Standards
- Follow Java naming conventions: test method names use should_doSomething_when_condition
- Use @DisplayName with plain-language descriptions for readable test output
- Apply final on test classes that should not be extended
- Limit test class size; split large classes by behaviour group, not alphabetically

## Test Organization
- Use @Nested inner classes to group related test cases and share setup within a class
- Use @Tag("unit"), @Tag("integration") etc. for cross-cutting categorization
- Register custom tags in junit-platform.properties to prevent unrecognized-tag warnings
- Keep test helper utilities in a dedicated src/test/java/support package

## Lifecycle and Setup
- Use @BeforeEach / @AfterEach for per-test setup and teardown
- Use @BeforeAll / @AfterAll (static) for expensive one-time setup (database, containers)
- Prefer @TestInstance(Lifecycle.PER_CLASS) when sharing state across tests is intentional
- Implement TestExecutionListener for cross-cutting concerns (logging, metrics)

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern in every test method
- Write single-responsibility tests: assert one logical outcome per test
- Keep tests independent; never rely on execution order or shared mutable state
- Use @Disabled with a reason and a tracking link, never silently skip tests
- Avoid catching exceptions in tests; use assertThrows instead

## Assertions
- Use org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions for standard checks
- Use assertAll() to group related assertions and report all failures at once
- Use assertThrows() and assertDoesNotThrow() for exception path testing
- Use assertTimeout() / assertTimeoutPreemptively() for time-sensitive assertions
- Consider AssertJ as an alternative for fluent, readable assertion chains

## Parameterized Tests
- Use @ParameterizedTest with appropriate source annotations:
  - @ValueSource for simple scalar inputs
  - @CsvSource / @CsvFileSource for tabular data
  - @MethodSource for complex object arguments from a static factory method
  - @EnumSource to exercise all enum values
- Provide a meaningful name attribute on @ParameterizedTest for readable output

## Extensions
- Use @ExtendWith to register JUnit 5 extensions (MockitoExtension, SpringExtension)
- Implement Extension interfaces (BeforeEachCallback, ParameterResolver) for reusable behaviours
- Register global extensions via ServiceLoader in META-INF/services for project-wide defaults
- Avoid mixing JUnit 4 @RunWith with JUnit 5 — use @ExtendWith exclusively

## Mocking
- Use Mockito with @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) for automatic mock lifecycle
- Use @Mock, @InjectMocks, and @Spy annotations instead of manual Mockito.mock() calls
- Verify interactions with verify() and verifyNoMoreInteractions() where meaningful
- Use ArgumentCaptor to assert on complex arguments passed to mocked methods

## Test Data Management
- Use factory methods or builder patterns to construct test objects
- Store shared test fixtures as static final constants or provide them via @MethodSource
- Use Faker or EasyRandom for generating realistic random domain objects
- Keep test data out of production code; never commit sensitive values

## Integration Testing
- Use Testcontainers for database, message broker, and external service dependencies
- Annotate slow integration tests with @Tag("integration") and exclude from fast builds
- Reset database state between tests with @Transactional rollback or container resets
- Use WireMock for HTTP dependency stubbing in integration tests

## Coverage
- Configure JaCoCo for coverage measurement in Maven (jacoco-maven-plugin) or Gradle
- Set minimum line/branch coverage thresholds to fail the build on regressions
- Exclude generated code, DTOs, and configuration classes from coverage requirements
- Publish HTML reports as CI artifacts for visual review

## Performance
- Use @Timeout at the method or class level to guard against runaway tests
- Run independent tests in parallel: set junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled=true
- Configure parallelism mode (same_thread, concurrent) per class with @Execution
- Profile test suite duration with --details=verbose to find slow outliers

## CI/CD Integration
- Use the Maven Surefire Plugin (3.x) or Gradle JUnit Platform support for test execution
- Generate Surefire XML reports for CI test result parsing
- Fail fast on first failure with -Dsurefire.failIfNoSpecifiedTests=false in PR pipelines
- Cache ~/.m2 or Gradle caches between CI runs to speed up dependency resolution

## Configuration
- Centralise JUnit Platform settings in src/test/resources/junit-platform.properties
- Use junit.jupiter.testmethod.order.default=MethodOrderer$DisplayName for stable ordering
- Enable parallel execution and configure pool size in junit-platform.properties
- Use Maven profiles or Gradle test tasks to toggle tag-based test subsets

## Documentation
- Add a brief Javadoc comment on complex test methods explaining the scenario
- Maintain a tests/README.md covering how to run subsets, custom tags, and integration tests
- Document custom extensions and their purpose in the support package

Remember to leverage JUnit 5's powerful extension model and native parameterization support for clean, expressive, and maintainable Java test suites.
